            <h4>Class Z Areas (Other Districts &amp; Rural)</h4>
            <div class="badge-tag">All Remaining 45+ Districts</div>
            <p><strong>Projected HRA Rate:</strong> 9% of Basic Pay.</p>
            <p><strong>Covered Areas:</strong> Udaipur, Alwar, Bhilwara, Sikar, Bharatpur, Pali, small towns, tehsils, and rural village schools.</p>
            <span class="card-footer-note">Baseline rate providing housing subsidies across rural field postings.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>ACP Rules (9, 18, 27 Years) vs Central MACP (10, 20, 30 Years)</h2>
        <p>
          Rajasthan operates a specialized career progression scheme that provides faster financial relief than the central system:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The 9, 18, and 27 Year ACP Rule:</h4>
          <p>
            While Central Government employees wait 10, 20, and 30 years for Modified Assured Career Progression (MACP), Rajasthan grants <strong>Assured Career Progression (ACP) at 9, 18, and 27 years</strong> of continuous regular service.
          </p>
          <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
            <li><strong>First ACP (9 Years):</strong> An employee completing 9 years without promotion advances to the next promotional Pay Level (e.g., L-4 moves to L-5, or L-10 moves to L-11).</li>
            <li><strong>Second ACP (18 Years):</strong> Advances to the second promotional level upon completing 18 years of service.</li>
            <li><strong>Third ACP (27 Years):</strong> Grants financial upgradation to the third promotional level upon completing 27 years.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Pay fixation under ACP awards one vertical 3% increment in the existing level before placing the employee into the equal or next higher cell of the upgraded level.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Pension Policy in Rajasthan: Old Pension Scheme (OPS) &amp; RGHS</h2>
        <p>
          Retirement compensation in Rajasthan carries significant policy importance:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
          <li><strong>The Old Pension Scheme (OPS) Framework:</strong> Rajasthan was among the earliest states to announce the restoration of the defined-benefit <strong>Old Pension Scheme (OPS)</strong> for employees recruited post-2004, halting mandatory National Pension System (NPS) market deductions and replacing them with General Provident Fund (GPF) accounts. The state workforce closely monitors whether the 8th CPC will interface with OPS or the newly designed <strong>Unified Pension Scheme (UPS)</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>State Pensioner Revisions:</strong> More than five lakh existing pensioners will see their baseline monthly pension multiplied by the approved fitment factor, elevating the statutory minimum state pension from ₹9,000 to approximately <strong>₹20,520 per month</strong> under the 2.28 factor.</li>
          <li><strong>Rajasthan Government Health Scheme (RGHS):</strong> State employees and pensioners benefit from cashless OPD and IPD medical coverage under RGHS, with standard monthly subscriptions deducted directly on electronic pay slips.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Digital Payroll Integration via IFMS &amp; PayManager Portals</h2>
        <p>
          Rajasthan administers its entire public wage disbursements through advanced digital accounting platforms:
        </p>
        <p>
          All salary bills, monthly pay slips (GA-55 statements), annual increments, GPF advances, and State Insurance (SI) deductions are processed electronically through the <strong>PayManager (paymanager.raj.nic.in)</strong> and <strong>Integrated Financial Management System (IFMS 3.0)</strong> networks.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once the 8th Pay Commission Government Orders are officially gazetted by the Finance (Rules) Department, revised basic pay matrices and retrospective arrear calculations will be automated within the electronic payroll network, ensuring rapid, direct-benefit bank transfers across all districts.
        </p>
      </section>

